hexagon: htp: reduce the rope angle with a split pi
Fold-into: upstream/hexagon-htp

Base: llama.cpp commit c6824a9 plus every patch of the series before this one.

hvx_vec_sincos_f32 (the sin and cos of the rope cache) reduces the angle x
to y = x - n pi, with pi rounded to f32 and the product n pi rounded to f32.
The error of y grows with n, thus with the position of the token. For the
Qwen3.5 rope (IMROPE, n_dims 64, base 1e7), the simulator of the v79 gives
these maximum errors of cos and sin against float64, at 256 positions each:

  position     512      4096     8192     32768    131072   262143
  error        4.8e-5   3.6e-4   5.0e-4   2.8e-3   1.1e-2   1.4e-2

The CPU and the oracle compute sinf and cosf of the same f32 angle, thus
their error does not grow with the position. The app graphs reach this
condition: each full attention layer turns Q and K by angles up to the
position of the token.

This patch reduces with Cody-Waite: pi is the sum of three parts of 7
significant bits and one f32 part. For n < 2^17 (all the positions of a
262144-token context), n times a 7-bit part is exact in f32. The simulator
then gives a maximum error of 1.8e-7 (sin) and 4.0e-7 (cos) at each of the
positions above. The reduction costs 12 more cycles for each vector of 32
angles (138 in place of 126), and the rope cache needs one vector for each
position of each thread.

diff --git a/ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/hvx-sin-cos.h b/ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/hvx-sin-cos.h
index 8648af0..9f4acf7 100644
--- a/ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/hvx-sin-cos.h
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/hvx-sin-cos.h
@@ -4,12 +4,21 @@
 #include "hvx-base.h"
 #include "hvx-floor.h"
 
+// Subtract n * part from y (the product and the difference are f32).
+static inline HVX_Vector hvx_vec_sincos_sub_part_f32(HVX_Vector y, HVX_Vector n, float part) {
+    HVX_Vector n_part = Q6_Vsf_equals_Vqf32(Q6_Vqf32_vmpy_VsfVsf(n, hvx_vec_splat_f32(part)));
+    return Q6_Vsf_equals_Vqf32(Q6_Vqf32_vsub_VsfVsf(y, n_part));
+}
+
 // Range-reduce x to y in [-pi/2, pi/2] and the quadrant sign (-1)^n.
 // Floor/truncate need IEEE bits, so convert qf32 back to sf before them.
+// The reduction is Cody-Waite: pi is the sum of three parts of 7 significant bits and one f32 part.
+// For n < 2^17 (|x| < 411774, the rope angles of a 262144-token context), n times a 7-bit part is
+// exact in f32, thus the reduced angle has an error of a few ulp of y. With pi rounded to f32 in
+// one product, the error grows with n (2.8e-3 at x = 32768).
 static inline void hvx_vec_sincos_reduce_f32(HVX_Vector x, HVX_Vector * y, HVX_Vector * sign) {
     HVX_Vector const_inv_pi  = hvx_vec_splat_f32(0.3183098861837907f);
     HVX_Vector const_half    = hvx_vec_splat_f32(0.5f);
-    HVX_Vector const_pi      = hvx_vec_splat_f32(3.141592653589793f);
     HVX_Vector const_one     = hvx_vec_splat_f32(1.0f);
     HVX_Vector const_neg_one = hvx_vec_splat_f32(-1.0f);
     HVX_Vector const_one_i   = Q6_V_vsplat_R(1);
@@ -20,8 +29,10 @@ static inline void hvx_vec_sincos_reduce_f32(HVX_Vector x, HVX_Vector * y, HVX_V
     HVX_Vector n_float = hvx_vec_floor_f32(x_over_pi);
     HVX_Vector n_int   = hvx_vec_truncate_f32(n_float);
 
-    HVX_Vector n_pi = Q6_Vsf_equals_Vqf32(Q6_Vqf32_vmpy_VsfVsf(n_float, const_pi));
-    *y = Q6_Vsf_equals_Vqf32(Q6_Vqf32_vsub_VsfVsf(x, n_pi));
+    HVX_Vector r = hvx_vec_sincos_sub_part_f32(x, n_float, 3.125f);
+    r = hvx_vec_sincos_sub_part_f32(r, n_float, 0.016357421875f);
+    r = hvx_vec_sincos_sub_part_f32(r, n_float, 0.0002346038818359375f);
+    *y = hvx_vec_sincos_sub_part_f32(r, n_float, 6.278329465203569e-07f);
 
     HVX_VectorPred is_odd = Q6_Q_vcmp_eq_VwVw(Q6_V_vand_VV(n_int, const_one_i), const_one_i);
     *sign = Q6_V_vmux_QVV(is_odd, const_neg_one, const_one);
